impl Specification {
    /// Fixed-budget retypes of the slot dispatcher chain.
    pub(super) fn add_slot_dispatch_at(&mut self) -> Result<(), SpecError> {
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_SLOT_DISPATCH_AT, "hnf3_app_residual_slot_dispatch_at: the slot dispatcher with its slot_res premise bound at a FIXED budget instead of universally quantified. \
\
The proof term is UNCHANGED -- all eight slot_res applications in the original already receive its own k0, never another budget, so only the binder moves. What forces the variant is the TYPE: the budget induction can construct slot_res at k and below, never at every budget at once, so the polymorphic premise is unsuppliable from inside the recursion. Added ALONGSIDE the original rather than replacing it; in-place edits to proved declarations are the failure mode that broke def_eq_fuel_of_struct earlier in this programme. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_REC_SLOT_NARROWED_AT, "hnf3_app_residual_rec_slot_narrowed_at: the same fixed-budget retype one level up, delegating to hnf3_app_residual_slot_dispatch_at. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_COVERED_AT, "hnf3_app_residual_covered_at: the ten-of-eleven residual coverage lemma, with its rec_res premise bound at a FIXED budget. Its single rec_res application already received its own k0, so the proof term is unchanged and only the binder moves. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_COVERED_SLOT_NARROWED_AT, "hnf3_app_residual_covered_slot_narrowed_at: the TOP of the fixed-budget chain, and the entry point the budget induction will actually apply. Delegates to hnf3_app_residual_covered_at and hnf3_app_residual_rec_slot_narrowed_at, both at the same fixed k0. \
\
This is the declaration that made the retype chain necessary: its body passes a residual INTO hnf3_app_residual_covered, so a fixed-budget slot_res could not be threaded through a polymorphic callee. The chain had to be retyped one level further up than first mapped. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_LE_OF_SUCC_LE, "le_of_succ_le: Le (Nat.succ a) b -> Le a b. The arithmetic step the coming spine induction needs in two of its branches, and the one piece of that chain that was neither proved nor in tree. \
\
le_succ_weaken exists and is the WRONG DIRECTION -- it weakens the RIGHT side (Le a c -> Le a (Nat.succ c)). Nothing supplied this one. \
\
Note the recursor convention, which cost a cycle: Le's FIRST argument is a PARAMETER, not an index, so Le.rec takes it first and the motive ranges over the second argument plus the proof -- Le.rec k (fun (j : Nat) (_hj : Le k j) => ...). Treating both as indices produces a motive-arity mismatch that reads as an opaque App/FVar error. With the parameter form the proof is two lines: refl gives Le a (succ a) by one step, and the step arm re-steps the induction hypothesis. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_NEUTRAL_SPINE, "iota_neutral_of_stuck_recursor_spine: THE LAST NEW LEMMA of the def-eq completeness chain. Every prefix of a stuck recursor spine is iota-neutral. \
\
WHY IT IS NEEDED, and why iota_immune_of_stuck_major does not suffice: nf_head.neutral at r = app zf za wants iota_neutral ZF -- the FUNCTION part -- and iota_neutral.app demands iota_immune at EVERY prefix, not just at r. iota_neutral_of_dead_const_head cannot supply it either: that lemma needs recmeta_for nm = none, and here nm CARRIES recmeta, since it is a recursor. That is the entire situation. \
\
The motive carries a two-case SlotOK condition in CPS alongside the head equation, because the two iota_immune suppliers apply under different conditions. The app arm eliminates the incoming SlotOK: if the whole prefix is under-applied, iota_immune_of_under_applied fires and the function part is under-applied too; if the slot is present, iota_immune_of_stuck_major fires with the same hno and SlotOK for the function part comes from splitting major_idx against its argument count with nat_le_trichotomy_t -- slot-inside recovers it with list_head_drop_append_some_inv, while the boundary and under-applied branches both give the Le. \
\
The const arm passes THE SAME PROOF TWICE: const_whnf n us is definitionally the delta-dead equation that iota_neutral.const also takes as its second premise. Seven arms discriminate on an absent head name. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_HNO_AT, "hno_at: the hno supplier AT A BUDGET, built from the induction hypothesis. The substantial half of the hnf3 assembly. \
\
Given a stuck recursor spine at budget k, produces the fact iota_immune_of_stuck_major needs: no reduct of the major is constructor-headed with a matching rule. Runs the FIVE-WAY inverter -- not the late collapse, whose kearly carries no data and so cannot refute levels 1-3. Each early level dies against a hypothesis the caller already holds: k1 against the head equation, k2 by identifying the rebound recname with nm1 and contradicting hrec, k3 by contradicting hslot. \
\
k5 and k6 hand over wh major = some wmajor, which whnf_fuel_red_wh3_result_stuck_lt converts into a STRICTLY SMALLER budget j and stuckness of wmajor there; the induction hypothesis at j then supplies nf_head wmajor, and hno_of_head_none / hno_of_head_no_rule close it. That is the descent the whole programme is built on. \
\
TWO TRAPS, both live here. The reduction maj =>* wmajor comes from whnf_fuel_red_wh3_par_HW, never the unsuffixed form, whose blanket hiota3 premise is FALSE and would make the result vacuous. And every continuation REBINDS recname and meta, so each is aligned with nm1 and meta1 by option_some_inj before use; using the rebound names directly would prove a statement about the wrong constant. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_SLOT_RES_AT, "slot_res_at: THE slot_res PREMISE, DISCHARGED at a fixed budget from the induction hypothesis. This is the premise hnf3's application residual has been open on for the whole programme. \
\
Assembles the three big results into nf_head.neutral: hno_at supplies the no-constructor-reduct fact, iota_immune_of_stuck_major turns it into iota-immunity of the spine, and iota_neutral_of_stuck_recursor_spine supplies iota_neutral of the FUNCTION part -- which is what nf_head.neutral actually wants and what the bridge alone does not give. \
\
The function part's SlotOK comes from splitting major_idx against its argument count: slot-inside recovers it with list_head_drop_append_some_inv, while the boundary and under-applied branches give the Le (the latter via le_of_succ_le). Everything is transported to the application form first and the result carried back along heq. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_HNF3_STUCK_ALL, "hnf3_stuck_all: every wh3-stuck term has a normal-form head, at EVERY budget. Strong induction on the budget via nat_strong_rec. \
\
The motive is Q k := forall r, wh3_stuck_at k r -> nf_head r, which lands in Type because nf_head does, so it feeds nat_strong_rec directly with no wrapper. The step hands hnf_conv3 the application case, which slot_res_at discharges from the induction hypothesis at the strictly smaller budget. Every other shape hnf_conv3 handles itself. \
\
No separate base case: at budget zero the pre-pass returns none, so whnf_fuel_red_wh3_result_stuck_lt's hypothesis is unsatisfiable inside slot_res_at and the recursor row discharges itself. The induction is uniform in k. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_HNF3_FULL, "hnf3_full: *** THE hnf3 PREMISE, PROVED. *** \
\
forall m e r, whnf_fuel_red_wh3 the_red_env m e = some r -> nf_head r. This is exactly the HNF3 hypothesis (hnf_discharge3.rs) that the def-eq completeness capstone has carried as an assumption. It is now a theorem. \
\
From a returning loop, whnf_fuel_red_wh3_result_stuck_lt exhibits a STRICTLY SMALLER budget at which the result is stuck -- the bound whose strictness was verified rather than assumed at the start of this work, because Le would have left the budget induction with no descent measure -- and hnf3_stuck_all supplies nf_head there. \
\
NOTE what this does and does not do: it discharges the HNF3 premise. The capstone is re-instantiated at it by def_eq_fuel_complete_wh3_final, registered immediately below. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        self.add_recursive_def(SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL, "def_eq_fuel_complete_wh3_final: completeness of the THREE-WAY fuelled conversion algorithm def_eq_fuel_wh3, RELATIVE TO NORMALISATION. \
\
If DefEq a b holds and both terms are Wh3NormAcc, the algorithm accepts them at some fuel. Both capstone premises are discharged in place -- hiotap3 by hiotap3_discharged, hnf3 by hnf3_full -- and the chain carries no axiom, Opaque leaf or unchecked declaration. \
\
*** THREE RETRACTIONS. An earlier version of this description claimed more than the theorem gives; each is corrected here rather than quietly dropped. *** \
\
(1) IT IS NOT ABOUT THE DEPLOYED CHECKER. def_eq_fuel_wh3 is an analysis artifact. This crate's own criterion names the TWO-WAY def_eq_fuel_wh as the deployed-faithful loop (hnf_conv.rs), and no fidelity gate connects wh3 to the shipping is_def_eq. Calling this 'completeness of the def-eq checker' overstates it. \
\
(2) THERE IS NO SOUNDNESS COUNTERPART FOR THIS LOOP. def_eq_fuel_wh_sound is about the TWO-way def_eq_fuel_wh and additionally takes the BLANKET hiota premise that this programme proved false, so it is vacuous. Grepping the crate for any soundness result over wh3 returns nothing. This theorem must NOT be cited as one half of a sound-and-complete pair. \
\
(3) Wh3NormAcc IS A REAL PREMISE, NOT MERELY AN INPUT, and it is STRICTLY STRONGER than 'the loop returns'. It is HEREDITARY over wbelow3_plus, whose sub arm is arbitrary subexpression descent (subexpr_step, including lam_body). A lambda whose body does not normalise refutes it even though the loop returns on the lambda immediately. The earlier wording 'satisfiable and satisfied wherever the loop returns' was FALSE and is retracted. What remains true is that it cannot be discharged universally -- the reflected calculus is untyped with Type:Type, where SN is false -- so relativising to it is necessary, not a weakness. \
\
*** SCOPE, which must travel with any public statement of this result: *** complete against DefEq, which has no eta, no proof irrelevance, no structure-eta, no universe conversion and no literal computation -- all of which the shipping is_def_eq implements. \
\
NON-VACUITY: def_eq_fuel_complete_wh3_final_witness instantiates this theorem at a concrete term carrying a concrete Wh3NormAcc value, so the statement is demonstrably not empty. DerivedProved, zero axiom_deps.")?;
        Ok(())
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    /// The premise must be fixed-budget: no `(k1 : Nat)` binder inside
    /// `slot_res`, and the stuckness hypothesis stated at the dispatcher's own
    /// `k0`. That is the entire point of these variants.
    #[test]
    fn test_slot_res_premise_is_fixed_budget() {
        for src in [SRC_SLOT_DISPATCH_AT, SRC_REC_SLOT_NARROWED_AT] {
            assert!(
                src.contains("(slot_res : forall (r1 : KExpr) (zf1 : KExpr)"),
                "slot_res must not quantify over the budget"
            );
            assert!(
                src.contains("wh3_stuck_at k0 r1"),
                "stated at the dispatcher's own budget"
            );
            assert!(!src.contains("(k1 : Nat)"), "no budget binder may survive");
        }
    }

    /// `k0` must be bound BEFORE `slot_res`, since the premise now mentions it.
    #[test]
    fn test_budget_is_bound_before_the_premise() {
        for src in [SRC_SLOT_DISPATCH_AT, SRC_REC_SLOT_NARROWED_AT] {
            let k0 = src.find("(k0 : Nat)").expect("binds k0");
            let sr = src.find("(slot_res :").expect("binds slot_res");
            assert!(k0 < sr, "k0 must precede slot_res");
        }
    }

    /// The top of the chain must delegate to the `_at` variants throughout.
    /// A single polymorphic callee left in the body reintroduces exactly the
    /// premise the budget induction cannot supply.
    /// The covered lemma binds `rec_res`, not `slot_res` — but it must be
    /// fixed-budget for the same reason, and by the same move.
    #[test]
    fn test_covered_rec_res_premise_is_fixed_budget() {
        assert!(
            SRC_COVERED_AT.contains("(rec_res : forall (r1 : KExpr) (zf1 : KExpr)"),
            "rec_res must not quantify over the budget"
        );
        assert!(SRC_COVERED_AT.contains("wh3_stuck_at k0 r1"));
        assert!(!SRC_COVERED_AT.contains("(k1 : Nat)"));
        let k0 = SRC_COVERED_AT.find("(k0 : Nat)").expect("binds k0");
        let rr = SRC_COVERED_AT.find("(rec_res :").expect("binds rec_res");
        assert!(k0 < rr, "k0 must precede rec_res");
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_top_of_chain_delegates_to_at_variants() {
        assert!(SRC_COVERED_SLOT_NARROWED_AT.contains("hnf3_app_residual_covered_at k0"));
        assert!(SRC_COVERED_SLOT_NARROWED_AT.contains("hnf3_app_residual_rec_slot_narrowed_at k0"));
    }

    /// `Le`'s first argument is a PARAMETER, so `Le.rec` takes it first and the
    /// motive ranges over the second argument plus the proof. Treating both as
    /// indices produces an opaque motive-arity error; this pins the convention.
    #[test]
    fn test_le_rec_uses_the_parameter_convention() {
        assert!(
            SRC_LE_OF_SUCC_LE.contains("Le.rec (Nat.succ a) (fun (y : Nat)"),
            "the parameter comes first and the motive takes one index"
        );
        assert!(
            !SRC_LE_OF_SUCC_LE.contains("le_succ_weaken"),
            "le_succ_weaken weakens the RIGHT side and does not apply here"
        );
    }

    /// The spine lemma must reach BOTH iota_immune suppliers — one per branch
    /// of the SlotOK it carries. Only one would mean the motive's two-case
    /// condition had collapsed, and the induction would not cover the spine.
    #[test]
    fn test_spine_reaches_both_immune_suppliers() {
        for lemma in ["iota_immune_of_under_applied", "iota_immune_of_stuck_major"] {
            assert!(
                SRC_NEUTRAL_SPINE.contains(lemma),
                "spine arm must use {lemma}"
            );
        }
        assert!(
            SRC_NEUTRAL_SPINE.contains("nat_le_trichotomy_t"),
            "the slot-present branch splits on major_idx against the arg count"
        );
    }

    /// The const arm passes the same proof twice: `const_whnf n us` IS the
    /// delta-dead equation `iota_neutral.const` also takes. If a second,
    /// different proof ever appears there, someone has manufactured a
    /// conversion that is not needed.
    #[test]
    fn test_const_arm_passes_one_proof_for_both_premises() {
        let dd = "Eq.substType (OptionType KExpr) (fun (W : OptionType KExpr)";
        assert!(
            SRC_NEUTRAL_SPINE.matches(dd).count() >= 2,
            "the same delta-dead term fills both iota_neutral.const premises"
        );
    }

    /// The reduction must come from the `_hw` variant. The unsuffixed
    /// `whnf_fuel_red_wh3_par` carries a blanket `hiota3` that is FALSE, and
    /// using it would make the whole completeness result vacuous — which every
    /// gate in this repo would pass.
    #[test]
    fn test_hno_at_uses_the_repaired_par_lemma() {
        assert!(
            SRC_HNO_AT.contains("whnf_fuel_red_wh3_par_hw hiotap3_discharged"),
            "must use the soundness-constrained variant"
        );
    }

    /// The five-way inverter, not the late collapse: `kearly` carries no data,
    /// so levels 1-3 could not be refuted from it.
    #[test]
    fn test_hno_at_uses_the_five_way_inverter() {
        assert!(SRC_HNO_AT.contains("iota_reduct_whc3_stuck_inv_type"));
        assert!(!SRC_HNO_AT.contains("stuck_late_inv"));
    }

    /// The descent: the smaller budget comes from the strict bound, and the
    /// induction hypothesis is applied there.
    #[test]
    fn test_hno_at_descends_to_a_smaller_budget() {
        assert!(SRC_HNO_AT.contains("whnf_fuel_red_wh3_result_stuck_lt"));
        assert!(
            SRC_HNO_AT.contains("ih j hj wmajor hst"),
            "the induction hypothesis must be used at the smaller budget j"
        );
    }

    /// slot_res_at must reach all three big results. Missing the spine lemma
    /// would mean nf_head.neutral got its iota_neutral from somewhere weaker,
    /// and missing hno_at would mean the immunity came from nowhere.
    #[test]
    fn test_slot_res_at_assembles_the_three_results() {
        for lemma in [
            "hno_at k ih",
            "iota_immune_of_stuck_major",
            "iota_neutral_of_stuck_recursor_spine",
            "nf_head.neutral",
        ] {
            assert!(
                SRC_SLOT_RES_AT.contains(lemma),
                "slot_res_at must use {lemma}"
            );
        }
    }

    /// `hnf3_full` must have EXACTLY the shape of the HNF3 premise, or it does
    /// not discharge it. Pinned because a near-miss here would look like
    /// success and be worth nothing.
    #[test]
    fn test_hnf3_full_matches_the_premise_shape() {
        for frag in [
            "(m : Nat) (e : KExpr) (r : KExpr)",
            "(whnf_fuel_red_wh3 the_red_env m e) (OptionType.some KExpr r)",
            ": nf_head r :=",
        ] {
            assert!(
                SRC_HNF3_FULL.contains(frag),
                "hnf3_full must contain {frag}"
            );
        }
    }

    /// The induction must run on the BUDGET via nat_strong_rec, and must
    /// consume the strict bound. Anything else has no descent measure.
    #[test]
    fn test_hnf3_inducts_on_the_budget() {
        assert!(SRC_HNF3_STUCK_ALL.contains("nat_strong_rec"));
        assert!(SRC_HNF3_STUCK_ALL.contains("slot_res_at kk ih"));
        assert!(SRC_HNF3_FULL.contains("whnf_fuel_red_wh3_result_stuck_lt"));
    }

    /// The capstone must carry NO hypotheses beyond its inputs. If either
    /// premise binder ever reappears, the completeness claim has silently
    /// regressed to a conditional one.
    #[test]
    fn test_capstone_has_no_remaining_premises() {
        assert!(
            SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL.contains("hiotap3_discharged hnf3_full"),
            "both premises must be discharged in place"
        );
        for premise in ["(hiotap3 :", "(hnf3 :"] {
            assert!(
                !SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL.contains(premise),
                "{premise} must not survive as a binder"
            );
        }
    }

    /// The surviving Wh3NormAcc binders are INPUTS, not assumptions, and must
    /// stay — completeness for a fuelled checker is necessarily relative to
    /// normalisation. Dropping them would be an overclaim.
    #[test]
    fn test_normalisation_inputs_are_retained() {
        assert!(SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL.contains("(acca : Wh3NormAcc a)"));
        assert!(SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL.contains("Wh3NormAcc b -> DefEqFuelAcceptsWh3 a b"));
    }

    #[test]
    fn test_sources_balanced_and_ascii() {
        for src in [
            SRC_SLOT_DISPATCH_AT,
            SRC_REC_SLOT_NARROWED_AT,
            SRC_COVERED_AT,
            SRC_COVERED_SLOT_NARROWED_AT,
            SRC_LE_OF_SUCC_LE,
            SRC_NEUTRAL_SPINE,
            SRC_HNO_AT,
            SRC_SLOT_RES_AT,
            SRC_HNF3_STUCK_ALL,
            SRC_HNF3_FULL,
            SRC_COMPLETE_FINAL,
        ] {
            let mut depth: i64 = 0;
            for ch in src.chars() {
                match ch {
                    '(' => depth += 1,
                    ')' => depth -= 1,
                    _ => {}
                }
                assert!(depth >= 0);
            }
            assert_eq!(depth, 0, "unbalanced parens");
            assert!(src.is_ascii());
        }
    }
}
